3.–(1) There is hereby established a body to be known as the National Council for Technical and Vocational Education and Training.
(2) The Council shall be a body corporate and shall-
(a) have perpetual succession and a common seal;
(b) in its corporate name, be capable of suing and being sued;
(c) be capable of purchasing or otherwise acquiring and of alienating or disposing of any movable or immovable property;
(d) have power to lend or borrow any sums of money which it may require for its purposes;
(e) be capable of entering into contracts; and
(f) have power to perform all other acts and things which are necessary for the proper performance of its functions or which may be performed under this Act.

5.–(1) The functions of the Council shall be to-
(a) register and accredit technical and vocational training institutions and centres capable of delivering courses;
(b) register technical and vocational teachers and other qualified technicians;
(c) assist technical and vocational training institutions and centres in the transmission of knowledge, principles and training in the field of technical and vocational education and training for the benefit of the people of Tanzania;
(d) assist technical and vocational training institutions and centres in the overall development of the quality of education they provide and to promote and to maintain approved academic standards;
(e) establish and make awards in technical and vocational education and training which are consistent in standard and comparable to related awards in Tanzania and internationally;
(f) ensure that the quality of education required for the award is met and maintained throughout the duration of the delivery of the course;
(g) assist technical and vocational training institutions and centres in their development by introducing and developing policies and procedures that will allow institutions to attain greater autonomy in the delivery of the courses;
(h) advise the Government on the planning and development of technical and vocational education and training including matters relating to manpower planning, staffing, budgetary and capital provision, the efficiency of the sector and the development of the curriculum;
(i) review technical and vocational education and training and training policies in the light of changing technologies and economic development; Powers and Council
(j) accept in partial fulfilment of a course of study leading to the Council’s awards such periods of learning and training as it may recognise as being equivalent in outcome to that award;
(k) co-operate with other bodies and organisations for such purposes as the Council may deem to be appropriate to achieve its objectives;
(l) advise the Minister on the grant of autonomy to any accredited technical college;
(m) appoint the members of its Constituent Boards and Committees;
(n) appoint moderators of its Constituent Boards and Committees;
(o) receive income and undertake expenditure as may be necessary or appropriate for the discharge of its functions;
(p) establish zonal offices in various regions within Tanzania for proper performance of its functions and serving the community at large;
(q) regulate vocational education and training including short tailor-made course programmes and in-service training; and
(r) introduce any measures and procedures which may be necessary for the furtherance of the objectives of the Council.

9. The functions of the Board shall be to-
(a) formulate, develop and renew curricular;
(b) validate courses for the awards of the Council;
(c) give guidance to technical and vocational training institutions and centres on the delivery of courses and the elements required to enhance their quality;
(d) determine the conduct and arrangements for assessment and examination; and
(e) recognise the Departments within accredited technical and vocational training institutions and centres and to deliver courses leading to the Council’s awards.

